import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"one language, many interpretations (the VM is the language)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"one language, many interpretations (the VM *is* the language)","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"proposed — **revised** (the first draft recommended porting the"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0010-—-one-language-many-interpretations-the-vm-is-the-language" tabindex="-1">ADR 0010 — one language, many interpretations (the VM <em>is</em> the language) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0010-—-one-language-many-interpretations-the-vm-is-the-language" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0010 — one language, many interpretations (the VM is the language)”">​</a></h1><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt the interpretation model: one object language, many engines.</strong></p><ol><li><p><strong>One language.</strong> A single <code>Language</code> value whose tables are <strong>data</strong>: <code>semantics</code> (kind → term), <code>types</code> (kind → shape/type rule), <code>shapes</code> (node shapes + named child roles), <code>transforms</code> (laws), and the cost/executor tables. No separate language for types, runtime or rewriting.</p></li><li><p><strong>Interpretations are engines over that data.</strong> <code>eval</code> (tensor execution), <code>types</code> (shape inference), <code>rewrite</code> (laws — the e-graph is <strong>one strategy</strong>, alongside simpler passes), <code>lower</code> (to the op-graph the sinks consume), <code>cost</code>. Each takes the <code>Language</code> and its own globals; none owns the syntax.</p></li><li><p><strong>The kernel is the shared term type.</strong> The arrow-calculus <code>Term</code> (composition, <code>⊗</code>, <code>Δ</code>/<code>dup</code>, <code>loop</code>/trace, <code>arr</code>, handlers) is what schemes compile <em>into</em> — one representation the interpretations agree on. This replaces ADR 0009&#39;s <code>Machine</code> port with a <strong><code>Calculus</code></strong> (the term/rule structure) plus per-interpretation entry points.</p></li><li><p><strong>Use <code>handl</code> <em>as</em> the kernel — do not reimplement it.</strong> The first draft proposed porting the pattern into Python; that was wrong. <code>handl</code> <strong>already is</strong> the arrow calculus (<code>arr</code>, <code>flow</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>axis_flow</code>, <code>loop</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>op</code>/<code>handle</code>, <code>graded_arrow</code>), its language is data (ADR-0044), and it has the engines — rewrite (with traversals, traces, stats), typecheck, parse, fmt, lint, test, and <strong>three</strong> exec strategies. Critically, it <strong>supports host functions</strong> (<code>crates/handl-runtime/src/vm_primitives.rs</code> — &quot;host functions for the exec model&#39;s VM instruction set&quot;; the conformance corpus has a case calling a foreign host function), so <strong>catopt&#39;s tensor primitives are host functions</strong>, not a reason to fork the kernel.</p><p>catopt then contributes only what is genuinely catopt&#39;s: the <strong>language fragments</strong> (laws as <code>TransformDef</code> rows, shapes, semantics tables), the <strong>host functions</strong> for tensor ops, the <strong>domain adapters</strong> (<code>catopt-torch</code>/<code>onnx</code>/<code>cuda</code>) and the <strong>evaluation dimension</strong> (cost, profiles, meters), plus discovery and RL. Precedent in this repository: <code>catopt-native</code> is already an optional Rust engine, excluded from the workspace, built with maturin — a Rust kernel is an established shape here, not a new kind of dependency.</p></li><li><p><strong>Laws become rows.</strong> A law is a <code>transforms</code> entry (data), not a Python constructor — the <em>data-only</em> principle, in a format that already has a proven engine behind it. This is what gives minted laws a versioning story (ADR 0009&#39;s standing gap).</p></li></ol><h3 id="why-this-is-a-prerequisite" tabindex="-1">Why this is a prerequisite <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-is-a-prerequisite" aria-label="Permalink to “Why this is a prerequisite”">​</a></h3><p>ADR 0009&#39;s calculus stage cannot be scoped without it: &quot;promote the calculus to a package&quot; presumes we know whether the calculus is <em>a language with interpretations</em> (this ADR) or <em>a second representation beside the op-graph</em> (0009&#39;s original framing, which risks the drift ADR 0008 warned about). Fix the model first; then the package boundary follows.</p><h2 id="why" tabindex="-1">Why <a class="header-anchor" href="#why" aria-label="Permalink to “Why”">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0009 asks whether the calculus needs a runtime VM, and its first draft implied <em>two machines</em> — a rewrite machine (apply 2-cells) and an evaluation machine (run 1-cells). That framing is wrong in a way that matters, because it implies <strong>separate languages</strong> for types, runtime and rewriting.</p><h2 id="not-doing" tabindex="-1">Not doing <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-doing" aria-label="Permalink to “Not doing”">​</a></h2><ul><li>none recorded — legacy record; the design doc lists what was deferred.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The &quot;laws are exclusively data&quot; rule becomes implementable</strong> — with a</li><li><strong>The e-graph stops being the architecture.</strong> It becomes one search</li><li><strong>New semantics = new schemes.</strong> Extending the vocabulary is a data</li><li><strong>catopt and handl share one idea</strong>, so a construct expressed in one</li></ul><h2 id="enforcement" tabindex="-1">Enforcement <a class="header-anchor" href="#enforcement" aria-label="Permalink to “Enforcement”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Code and tests are named in the design doc.</li></ul><h2 id="design" tabindex="-1">Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#design" aria-label="Permalink to “Design”">​</a></h2><p>The full record (context, tables, phases, trade-offs): <a href="./../design/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.html"><code>design/0010-one-language-many-interpretations.md</code></a>.</p>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};